\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Reflected transports in the centered and Wilson operators}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the \vocabk{spinor time map}{fdip-017Z}, index a
\vocabk{connection}{fdip-006D} by the positive steps.
Use the \vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002}
with vertices #{\Lambda} and one edge #{(x,j)} from #{x}
to #{x+e_j} for every site and direction, where #{e_j}
is one step along direction #{j}. Its represented
unitary transport is #{U_j(x)}. For #{N_j\ge3} these are
the positive nearest-neighbor edges of the
[covariant lattice Dirac operator](fdip-009O). For #{N_j=2},
#{(x,j)} and #{(x+e_j,j)} are distinct edges with independent
links, not formal reverses of one another. For #{N_j=1},
the edge is a loop; reversing that edge inverts its transport.
The chosen time direction is reversed as a labeled direction,
even when its two signed site images agree, as in a
\vocabk{period-preserving signed permutation}{fdip-0164}.}

\p{At spacing #{a>0} use the shifts and operators
##{\begin{aligned}
(T_j^U\psi)(x)&=(\bbI_S\otimes U_j(x)^{-1})\psi(x+e_j),\\
D_{a,U}&=\frac1{2a}\sum_j(\varepsilon_j\otimes\bbI_F)
   \bigl(T_j^U-(T_j^U)^{-1}\bigr),\\
L_{a,U}&=a^{-2}\sum_j \bigl(T_j^U+(T_j^U)^{-1}-2\bbI\bigr).
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{-L_{a,U}\ge0}. With a real coefficient #{c_W} put
#{D_{W,a,U}=D_{a,U}-c_W L_{a,U}} as for the
[Wilson operator](fdip-009U).}

\p{Let #{b_F(u)=c_F^{-1}u c_F} for a represented fiber
unitary #{u}. The reflected positive links are
##{\begin{aligned}
 U'_j(x)&=b_F(U_j(\theta_s x)) &&(j\ne t),\\
 U'_t(x)&=b_F(U_t(\theta_s x-e_t)^{-1}).
\end{aligned}}
Conjugation by #{S_t} takes #{T_j^U} to #{T_j^{U'}} for
#{j\ne t} and takes #{T_t^U} to #{(T_t^{U'})^{-1}}. For
every one of #{D_{a,U},L_{a,U},D_{W,a,U}}, it gives the
same operator with #{U'} in place of #{U} and the same
coefficient. The new links take values in the image of the
represented group under #{b_F}.}

\proof{
\p{The inverse shift uses #{U_j(x-e_j)} on the value at
#{x-e_j}. This follows by composing the forward shift with
that formula. Each shift is unitary by reindexing the norm
sum and using unitary links. Hence
#{2\bbI - T_j^U-(T_j^U)^{-1}=(\bbI - T_j^U)^\dagger
(\bbI - T_j^U)}, proving the sign of the Laplacian.}

\p{Conjugation by #{\mathcal Q^{-1}} changes each transport
#{u} to #{b_F(u)}. The last spin multiplier commutes with
fiber maps. Pullback fixes the direction of spatial steps, so
the transformed forward spatial coefficient is
#{b_F(U_j(\theta_s x)^{-1})=U'_j(x)^{-1}}. For the time
step the reflected forward action reads at #{x} the value
at #{x-e_t}, with coefficient #{b_F(U_t(\theta_s x)^{-1})}.
That coefficient must be #{U'_t(x-e_t)} in the inverse
target shift. Since #{\theta_s(x-e_t)=\theta_s x+e_t},
the displayed time link gives exactly this coefficient.
Reflection sends the positive time step from #{x} to the formal
reverse of the positive step based at #{\theta_s x-e_t},
the reflected head of the original edge, as in \ref{fdip-00PY}.
Spatial steps keep their positive orientation. These rules
also apply to parallel steps and loops.
All the reindexings above are bijections at every positive
period, so the same coefficient identities hold there.}

\p{The inverse spin antiunitary commutes with all directions.
Conjugation by #{\varepsilon_t} fixes #{\varepsilon_t} and
negates each spatial direction, while #{\Gamma} negates every
direction. Their product #{\Gamma\varepsilon_t} thus negates
only the time direction. In the time summand of #{D_{a,U}}
both the direction and the difference change sign, giving
the positive centered covariance. The Laplacian uses a sum
invariant under interchanging a shift and its inverse. The
Wilson combination follows because #{c_W} is real.
Normalization of the represented group by #{c_F} keeps the
reflected transports in that group. A connection is fixed when
its transformed links equal its original links. An identification
of two connections must intertwine their represented transports.}
}
